## Organizations (multi-tenancy)

This service is multi-tenant: every authenticated user can create **organizations** and
each `(organization, user)` pair is a **membership** carrying a `role`. The `/orgs`
router is the example surface:

```text
POST   /orgs                          create an org (caller becomes its owner)
GET    /orgs                          list the orgs the caller belongs to
GET    /orgs/{org_id}/members         list members (caller must belong to the org)
POST   /orgs/{org_id}/members         add a member
DELETE /orgs/{org_id}/members/{user}  remove a member
```

The `Organization` and `Membership` models live in `app.tenancy.models`; the per-membership
`role` is a free string (`owner` / `admin` / `member` by convention) ready to be enforced by
a permissions layer.

{% if include_rbac %}
### Roles & permissions (RBAC)

The `app.rbac` package turns that `role` into enforcement. `ROLE_RANK` orders the roles
(`owner > admin > member` — extend by adding one) and `SCOPES_BY_ROLE` maps each role to
permission scopes. Two FastAPI dependency factories guard routes:

- `require_role("admin")` — the caller must hold at least that role in the org, else `403`.
- `require_scope("members:write")` — the caller's role must grant that scope, else `403`.

They ship applied as the example: adding a member requires the `admin` role and removing a
member requires the `members:write` scope, so a plain `member` is denied both.

